"""ShapePipe real-data orchestration — Snakemake workflow.

Design / rationale: CosmoStat/shapepipe#848, D1-D5.

`sp run` uses two Snakemake invocations over this Snakefile:

    SP_PHASE=prepare snakemake prepare_all_tiles   # Git -> Uz -> Fe, per tile
    SP_PHASE=compute snakemake all                 # everything else

They are two because the exposure job set is *data-derived*: it comes from the
tiles' find_exposures output, and a Snakemake DAG is fixed at parse time. The
join between them is the tile<->exposure index, built by build_index.build()
at parse time of the compute invocation. The head process loads it into dicts;
job parses query only the edges they need. The index is not a rule input, so
rebuilding it changes the job set without itself invalidating completed work.
It accumulates across invocations so clean_exposure sees every consuming tile
in the campaign, not just the current tile list.

`sp run` points Snakemake at a campaign code snapshot, so `workflow.basedir`
below is that snapshot. See bin/sp for snapshot creation and reuse.

ShapePipe stage rules run one `shapepipe_run` on one unit (D2). Most declare a
success manifest (`<unit dir>/manifests/<stage>.json`) written by
`completeness.py check`, rather than per-CCD products: missing CCDs can be
legitimate, and per-CCD declarations would mean millions of paths at DR6 scale.
See rules/tile.smk for temporary chunk outputs and final-catalogue publication.

Stage logs (`<unit dir>/logs/<stage>.json`) retain failure evidence separately
from success manifests; see scripts/completeness.py for the verdict contract.
"""

import fnmatch
import functools
import hashlib
import json
import os
import sqlite3
import sys
from pathlib import Path

from snakemake.exceptions import WorkflowError
# Import the logger explicitly for parse-time warnings.
